ighs, 2 daily record lows","records":"Salina has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Salina's nights warmed over the long run. Its days have warmed since 1983.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 49\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13: a typical high, but a cold night","p_yest":"The high of <strong>49\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 57\u00b0F, and the record is 83\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>27\u00b0F</strong> was the 25th coldest November 13 low (tied) of 123 since 1900.","t_rec":"Salina has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Salina's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Salina has set <strong class=\"sig\">33 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 78 that chance alone would give, and <strong>92 record highs</strong>, close to the 78 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (94). Salina's all-time high is 118\u00b0F, on August 13, 1936.","t_chg":"Salina's nights warmed over the long run. Its days have warmed since 1983.","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Salina's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.25), about +2.1\u00b0F since 1900. Salina's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.09) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1983, lows show no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.81\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.35 to +1.27). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 6 of 12 months. Highs rose in none.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Salina's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.18 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 29.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1993 (58.08 in); the driest, 1966 (12.98 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}